                <text x="223" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">9</text>
                <text x="267" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">3</text>

                <!-- Chunk 3 -->
                <rect x="304" y="118" width="140" height="34" rx="2" fill="rgba(184,168,138,0.1)" stroke="#b8a88a" stroke-width="0.6"/>
                <text x="323" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">0</text>
                <text x="367" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">2</text>
                <text x="411" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">8</text>
                <text x="435" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Playfair Display, serif" font-size="16" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.85">6</text>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="illustration-caption">10 items exceeds working memory. 3 chunks does not. The digits are identical. The load is not.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="prose">
          This is the principle of <strong>chunking</strong> — one of the most important concepts in cognitive psychology, and one of the most underused in design. A chunk is a meaningful group of items that working memory can treat as a single unit. Ten individual digits exhaust working memory almost immediately. The same ten digits, grouped into three clusters, become three items — well within capacity. The information has not changed. The cognitive cost of holding it has been reduced by an order of magnitude.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          Chunking is not a trick. It is how the mind naturally organises information when the environment permits it. Proximity does the chunking automatically — elements that are near each other are grouped into a single unit in working memory. Similarity reinforces it. Clear boundaries make it explicit. <strong>Every act of visual grouping on a well-designed page is, in cognitive terms, a reduction in load.</strong> The designer's job is not to add information. It is to make the existing information holdable.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          Miller's Law — the 7 ± 2 limit — is not a suggestion. It is a description of hardware. No amount of good design can expand working memory beyond its biological limit. What design can do is <strong>ensure that the number of items a reader must hold at any one time stays within it</strong>. This is not an aspiration. It is the baseline requirement for comprehension.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <!-- ─── III. SCALE ─── -->
        <h2 id="scale">III. Scale</h2>

        <p class="prose">
          A system that works at small scale does not automatically work at large scale. This is not a design opinion. It is a mathematical fact about how cognitive load behaves as complexity increases.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          Load does not scale linearly with the number of elements. Double the elements on a page and the load more than doubles — because each new element does not only occupy working memory itself. It also creates new <strong>relationships</strong> that the mind must process: how does this element relate to the ones already present? Where does it sit in the hierarchy? Does it belong to the group above it, or does it start a new group? Each relationship is itself a cognitive item. The load of a system grows not with its size, but with its <em>interconnectedness</em>.
        </p>

