                <rect x="20" y="84" width="100" height="10" rx="1" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.06"/>
                <rect x="20" y="96" width="60" height="4" rx="1" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.04"/>

                <rect x="6" y="106" width="128" height="22" rx="2" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.13"/>
                <line x1="14" y1="132" x2="14" y2="148" stroke="#b8a88a" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                <rect x="20" y="132" width="90" height="10" rx="1" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.06"/>
                <rect x="20" y="144" width="70" height="4" rx="1" fill="#1a1a18" opacity="0.04"/>
              </svg>
              <div class="triple-label">25 items grouped<br>Load restored</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="illustration-caption">Same 25 items in three states. Flat at scale, the system breaks. Hierarchy does not reduce the information — it reduces what must be held at once.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="prose">
          This is why <strong>hierarchy is not a design preference. It is a cognitive necessity.</strong> Hierarchy does not reduce the total amount of information in a system. It reduces the amount of information that is <em>visible</em> — and therefore the amount that must be held in working memory — at any given moment. A site with 500 pages, organised into 5 sections of 100, presents the reader with 5 items at the top level. The 495 others exist, but they are not competing for working memory until they are needed.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          This is also why flat structures break at scale. A navigation with 5 items works because 5 is within Miller's limit. The same navigation with 25 items fails — not because 25 is an unusually large number, but because 25 exceeds the capacity of working memory to hold and compare simultaneously. The reader can no longer scan and find. They must search. And searching is cognitively expensive in a way that scanning is not.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          The relationship between scale and load is not a problem that can be solved by making things smaller, faster, or more visually distinct. It can only be solved structurally — by <strong>organising information into layers that the mind can navigate one at a time</strong>. This is the single most important structural insight in information design. Everything else — typography, spacing, colour, illustration — is refinement. Hierarchy is the foundation.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <!-- ─── IV. THE BREAKING POINT ─── -->
        <h2>IV. The Breaking Point</h2>

        <p class="prose">
          There is a moment in any system where load crosses its threshold. It is not always visible as chaos. Sometimes it looks like calm — a page that appears organised but requires more effort to read than it should. The elements are there. The hierarchy exists, on paper. But something in the arrangement exceeds what working memory can hold, and the reader feels it as <strong>a subtle, persistent exhaustion</strong>.
        </p>

        <!--
          ILLUSTRATION 4: THE BREAKING POINT
          A single horizontal illustration showing density increasing left to right.
          Left: sparse, ordered elements. Centre: the threshold — still readable but
          taut. Right: past the threshold — the same density but now the elements
          have begun to lose their relationships. Order dissolves.
        -->
        <div class="illustration-block">
          <div class="illustration-frame" style="max-width: 560px;">
            <svg width="500" height="120" viewBox="0 0 500 120" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">

