          </div>
          <p class="illustration-caption">Left to right: within capacity, at the threshold, past it. The elements do not change. The mind's ability to hold them does.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="prose">
          Designing for cognitive load is not about making things simple. Simplicity is a property of the content. It is about making things <strong>holdable</strong> — ensuring that at every point in the reader's experience, the number of items competing for working memory stays within the limit that biology has set. This requires constant vigilance about what is visible at any given moment. Not what exists on the page in total — but what the eye encounters simultaneously.
        </p>

        <p class="prose">
          This is why the doctrine governing this site specifies few page types, deliberate hierarchy, and low interaction density. It is not aesthetic conservatism. It is <strong>cognitive hygiene</strong> — a set of structural commitments made in full awareness of how human working memory operates, and in full respect of its limits.
        </p>

        <!-- CLOSING -->
        <footer class="essay-closing">
          <p class="prose">
            Human Patterns described what the mind responds to. Gestalt & Perception described how it responds. This essay has described how much it can hold. Together, they form a complete account — not of what design should look like, but of what the mind requires in order to perceive clearly. Everything else follows from that.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-signature">
            — LALINE, on the limits that govern all structure
          </p>
